1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,530
Chai Corner is busier than ever.

2
00:00:02,830 --> 00:00:05,884
But one morning, Kabir's
cash counter breaks down.

3
00:00:06,434 --> 00:00:09,355
Customers can't pay, so
the line stops moving.

4
00:00:09,655 --> 00:00:11,285
Asha's chai goes cold.

5
00:00:11,285 --> 00:00:13,466
Meena runs out of clean cups.

6
00:00:13,767 --> 00:00:17,005
One small part fails, and
the whole stall suffers.

7
00:00:17,655 --> 00:00:20,341
Chai Corner is not six separate jobs.

8
00:00:20,341 --> 00:00:21,769
It is one system.

9
00:00:22,469 --> 00:00:24,675
This idea came from biology.

10
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:29,651
Ludwig von Bertalanffy, an Austrian
biologist, studied living things as

11
00:00:29,651 --> 00:00:30,551
systems.

12
00:00:30,803 --> 00:00:34,730
In your body, the heart, lungs
and brain all need each other.

13
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:36,117
No part works alone.

14
00:00:36,467 --> 00:00:41,765
He called it general system theory,
and wrote about it from the 1940s.

15
00:00:42,115 --> 00:00:47,683
In 1966, Daniel Katz and Robert Kahn
applied it to organisations, in a

16
00:00:47,683 --> 00:00:48,875
famous book.

17
00:00:49,525 --> 00:00:51,616
So, what is the systems approach?

18
00:00:51,916 --> 00:00:56,917
It sees an organisation as one system
of connected parts, all working toward

19
00:00:56,917 --> 00:00:57,817
one goal.

20
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:00,260
Each part is called a subsystem.

21
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:03,282
That means a smaller
system inside the big one.

22
00:01:03,632 --> 00:01:08,521
At Chai Corner, the kitchen, the cash
counter and the cleaning are subsystems.

23
00:01:08,521 --> 00:01:10,180
Ravi links them together.

24
00:01:10,830 --> 00:01:13,195
Every system runs on the same loop.

25
00:01:13,195 --> 00:01:14,797
Let's watch one morning.

26
00:01:15,147 --> 00:01:16,047
Inputs.

27
00:01:15,805 --> 00:01:16,882
What goes in.

28
00:01:16,882 --> 00:01:19,943
Milk, tea leaves, sugar,
and people's time.

29
00:01:20,293 --> 00:01:21,193
Process.

30
00:01:21,034 --> 00:01:23,341
Asha turns the inputs into chai.

31
00:01:23,341 --> 00:01:25,419
This is called transformation.

32
00:01:25,769 --> 00:01:26,669
Outputs.

33
00:01:26,514 --> 00:01:27,753
What comes out.

34
00:01:27,753 --> 00:01:30,178
Hot chai, and customers who pay.

35
00:01:30,528 --> 00:01:31,428
Feedback.

36
00:01:31,272 --> 00:01:33,690
Customers say the chai is too sweet.

37
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,405
So next morning, Asha adds less sugar.

38
00:01:36,405 --> 00:01:39,725
What comes out, comes back,
and improves what goes in.

39
00:01:40,375 --> 00:01:42,744
Chai Corner is also an open system.

40
00:01:42,744 --> 00:01:45,220
It is linked to the world around it.

41
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:49,574
Customers, suppliers, the government,
and competitors all push on it.

42
00:01:49,924 --> 00:01:52,625
On a festival evening, the queue doubles.

43
00:01:52,625 --> 00:01:56,073
Ravi moves Kabir to serving,
and calls in extra help.

44
00:01:56,423 --> 00:01:59,456
A closed system has no
link to the outside.

45
00:01:59,456 --> 00:02:01,942
Real businesses never work that way.

46
00:02:02,592 --> 00:02:03,875
One more big word.

47
00:02:03,875 --> 00:02:04,775
Synergy.

48
00:02:04,881 --> 00:02:08,180
It means the whole is greater
than the sum of its parts.

49
00:02:08,530 --> 00:02:11,820
Working alone, six people
serve sixty cups an hour.

50
00:02:11,820 --> 00:02:14,181
As one team, they serve a hundred.

51
00:02:14,831 --> 00:02:17,904
So what does systems thinking
look like at work today?

52
00:02:18,254 --> 00:02:19,169
Subsystems.

53
00:02:19,169 --> 00:02:21,901
Every department is part of the whole.

54
00:02:21,901 --> 00:02:25,218
A hospital has emergency,
surgery and pharmacy.

55
00:02:25,568 --> 00:02:26,725
Interdependence.

56
00:02:26,725 --> 00:02:28,110
One delay spreads.

57
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:31,652
If a parts supplier is
late, a car factory stops.

58
00:02:32,002 --> 00:02:32,902
Feedback.

59
00:02:32,766 --> 00:02:34,153
Learn from results.

60
00:02:34,153 --> 00:02:38,183
Apps read user ratings, and fix
problems in the next update.

61
00:02:38,533 --> 00:02:39,530
Open system.

62
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:41,268
Watch the world outside.

63
00:02:41,268 --> 00:02:44,115
Shops stock up before the festival season.

64
00:02:44,465 --> 00:02:45,365
Synergy.

65
00:02:45,254 --> 00:02:47,022
Teams beat solo stars.

66
00:02:47,022 --> 00:02:51,401
Doctors, nurses and therapists
plan a patient's care together.

67
00:02:52,051 --> 00:02:54,571
So, is the systems approach enough?

68
00:02:54,571 --> 00:02:55,728
Not by itself.

69
00:02:56,078 --> 00:02:57,129
The good side.

70
00:02:57,129 --> 00:03:01,101
It shows the big picture, and how
every part affects the others.

71
00:03:01,451 --> 00:03:02,540
The hard side.

72
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:04,276
It can feel too abstract.

73
00:03:04,276 --> 00:03:07,699
It says everything is
connected, but not what to do.

74
00:03:08,049 --> 00:03:12,026
It gives managers a way to
see, not a step by step recipe.

75
00:03:12,376 --> 00:03:15,937
So the lesson is: see the whole
system, then fix the right part.

76
00:03:16,587 --> 00:03:17,569
Quick check.

77
00:03:17,569 --> 00:03:21,510
Ravi reads what customers say,
and changes tomorrow's recipe.

78
00:03:21,510 --> 00:03:23,860
Which part of the system is this?

79
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:26,660
Feedback!

80
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:30,609
Information from the output comes
back, and improves the system.

81
00:03:31,209 --> 00:03:32,222
So, remember.

82
00:03:32,522 --> 00:03:37,592
An organisation is one system, made
of connected parts called subsystems.

83
00:03:37,942 --> 00:03:42,401
Every system runs on a loop: inputs,
process, outputs, and feedback.

84
00:03:42,751 --> 00:03:46,530
It is open to the world, and the
whole is greater than its parts.

85
00:03:47,130 --> 00:03:52,042
Next time, we'll see why there is no one
best way to manage, in the contingency

86
00:03:52,042 --> 00:03:52,942
approach.
